00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:10.570
On February 28th, 2017, you might’ve thought
the internet was down.

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:16.130
4 hours without Netflix, Spotify, Buzzfeed,
Reddit, Dropbox, Pinterest, Imgur, League,

00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:18.150
Tinder, and thousands of others,

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:19.860
Even the site that reports outages.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.240
That’s embarrassing.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.190
If the world was more productive that day,
now we know why.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:27.070
r/Outside become, just, ya know, outside.

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:32.920
A shark hadn’t bitten an underwater cable,
nor was it five/nine, Just an Amazon engineer’s

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:33.920
typo.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:38.469
Probably a stressful afternoon in Seattle,
but also an impressive demonstration of the

00:00:38.469 --> 00:00:39.750
company’s size and power:

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:44.921
Amazon Web Services hosts so much of the internet
that for many people, myself included, it

00:00:44.921 --> 00:00:46.800
basically is the internet.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.510
We know Amazon as an online store, Companies
store their products in its warehouses, which

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:54.010
handle the marketing, and shipping, and returns.

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:59.989
For us, this means total convenience - one
click away from $125, 27-pound gummy bear

00:00:59.989 --> 00:01:03.629
pythons, or 5, crisp, 2 dollar bills for $20.

00:01:03.629 --> 00:01:05.640
Wait, that's not how money works…

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:10.030
A hundred thousand companies make over a hundred
thousand dollars a year this way.

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:13.020
But for Amazon, it’s only a fraction of
their business.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:17.970
There’s also Twitch, Whole Foods, Kindle,
Alexa sensibly named Echo, Echo Plus, Echo

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:23.340
Dot, Echo Dot Kids, Amazon Tap, Echo Connect,
Echo Spot, Echo Show, and Echo Look, also

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:29.290
a completely different Alexa, Fire Tablets
and TV, Prime Music, Video, Pantry, Ring Doorbell,

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:35.729
Zappos, IMDb, Fresh, GoodReads, and over 70
consumer brands you’d never know they owned.

00:01:35.729 --> 00:01:36.729
whew.

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:40.800
Hardly a month goes by where they don’t
enter and dominate a new industry,

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.109
Just trademarking the slogan “We do the
prep.

00:01:43.109 --> 00:01:46.880
You be the chef.” was enough to drop Blue
Apron’s stock 12%.

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.040
They’re even investigating pharmaceuticals,
education, and finance.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:56.159
Amazon defines its competitors as “publishers,
producers, and distributors of physical, digital,

00:01:56.159 --> 00:02:00.719
and interactive media of all types and all
distribution channels”, among others.

00:02:00.719 --> 00:02:02.420
That’s, like, everyone.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:05.990
Which raises the question: is Amazon… scatter-brained?

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:08.290
Many of these products have nothing in common.

00:02:08.290 --> 00:02:10.780
Yesterday they wanted to conquer streaming
video.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:13.470
Today, sell organic grapes in grocery stores.

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:15.330
Tomorrow, who knows?

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:19.690
And their ideas, increasingly let’s say,
creative: 2-day delivery?

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:25.570
How about 2-hour delivery, a 3D smartphone,
a grocery store without employees, a front

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:29.830
door that unlocks for delivery drivers, A
flying warehouse complete with detachable

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:30.830
drones.

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:35.290
For all its success as an online store, more
and more, it also seems distracted.

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:37.330
At least, that’s how it looks.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:41.600
The only way to make sense of their actions
and mistakes, and anticipate their future,

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.580
is to see the world as they do.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:48.680
And there are three pillars to Amazon’s
plan for world domination:

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:54.260
To really understand Amazon you have to understand Jeff Bezos

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:59.040
Like Steve Jobs or Elon Musk, the philosophy
of the man is that of the company.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.870
Apple was founded by people in love with technology
and its design.

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:08.230
No matter how big the company gets, this will
always be reflected in its decisions, priorities,

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:09.230
even mistakes.

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:13.000
Amazon began as a bookstore, but that was
never its heart and soul.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:14.000
or spine

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.840
Bezos chose books because no one bookstore
could hold all of them, warehouses visited

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:20.380
on the internet could.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:24.870
But make no mistake: They aren’t a book
company, a website, a delivery network, or

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:26.160
even a retailer.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:28.230
Amazon is a scale company.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:33.040
Bezos understood that when you take something
and multiply it a hundred, thousand, million

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:37.340
times, you can do things all the small businesses
in the world never could.

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:39.070
A tree is a tree.

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:44.480
But put 400 billion together and you have
the Amazon rainforest, a force so powerful

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.870
it controls the world’s climate.

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:50.320
From any other company, this sounds like generic
business-speak.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:52.310
But Amazon really means it.

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:58.400
We say a company is focused if it specializes
in beverages, or cars, or bad website design,

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:01.150
and puts all its XP into that ability.

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:05.730
Amazon is rare in that its specialty isn’t
the product itself but its scale.

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:07.070
That’s the focus.

00:04:07.070 --> 00:04:11.270
When considering a new product, the flowchart
is pretty simple: “Would this benefit from

00:04:11.270 --> 00:04:13.960
being a thousand times quicker, bigger, easier?”

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:17.890
If so, you can bet Amazon either sells it,
or soon will.

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:21.480
It’s easy to stop there, Sit back and enjoy
the profit.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:24.280
But Amazon asks “Okay, now what can we do?”

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:27.470
And this is why it’s unstoppable: the snowball
effect.

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:30.100
First, get as many users as possible.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:35.290
Give out $50 tablets, free shipping, license
Echo to every company willing.

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:38.940
More users bring more data, which helps improve
the product.

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:41.320
And the better product attracts even more
users.

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:46.100
They aren’t just making it easy to live
off Amazon, they’re making it hard not to.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:49.740
That’s the power of data in the hands of
someone operating at this scale.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:52.130
It’s why there’s now a movement to limit
this,

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:56.960
why companies like Digi.me, who I’ve previously
mistaken for a data collection company, actually

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.720
let users manage and safeguard their information.

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:04.240
And the results are things like Prime: It
may lose money on the heaviest shoppers, but

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:08.250
with a hundred million of them, they’re
winning a lot more than they’re losing.

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:11.380
So why purchase competitors like Whole Foods?

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:13.710
Exactly because it’s not what they’re
good at:

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:16.170
Books never expire, well, some do.

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:17.990
But groceries, not so much.

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:21.520
You have to go back repeatedly, putting Amazon
in your routine.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:23.160
And with so much shipping,

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:29.139
Spending $11 billion fulfilling 300 million
packages in 2015, they can do something almost

00:05:29.139 --> 00:05:32.760
no-one else can: Compete with UPS and FedEx.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:39.110
They already lease 32 Boeing 767 cargo jets,
and plan a massive cargo hub in Kentucky,

00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:43.620
But that’s just the beginning.

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:46.860
After announcing a new $79 Kindle, Bezos wrote

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:51.120
“There are two types of companies: those
that work hard to charge customers more, and

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:53.940
those that work hard to charge customers less.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:55.340
Both approaches can work.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:57.020
We are firmly in the second camp.”

00:05:57.020 --> 00:05:58.730
And he’s really not kidding…

00:05:58.730 --> 00:06:04.370
The parts alone cost $78.59, plus $5.66 for
assembly.

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:10.639
That’s a loss of $5.25 for every Kindle
sold, not including things like marketing,

00:06:10.639 --> 00:06:12.190
licensing, and support.

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:15.340
And sure, there are ads, but only as an option,

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:19.360
Companies like Facebook and YouTube are fundamentally
advertising companies.

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:24.490
No matter how well-intentioned YouTube employees
are, unless something drastic changes, the

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:28.190
company will always prioritize advertisers
over creators.

00:06:28.190 --> 00:06:30.030
That’s the business model.

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:34.180
And then there are customer companies - where
You and I decide what gets demonetized.

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:37.910
Apple is comfortable charging more for †he
very best experience,

00:06:37.910 --> 00:06:42.410
And for Amazon, helping the customer means
making us pay as little as possible.

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:46.050
Both are loved in a way that’s impossible
for an advertising company.

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:49.600
You might say - “They only care about the
customer to make more money”

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:53.730
And maybe you’re right, there’s no way
to know, but the effects are the same,

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:56.850
When Apple refused to unlock the San Bernardino
iPhone,

00:06:56.850 --> 00:06:59.610
When Amazon takes a loss for the sake of our
wallets,

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:02.660
and offers some of the best customer support
I’ve ever had,

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:06.670
It may only be a calculated business decision,
But it’s great for us.

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:11.050
Of course, when the customer comes first,
everyone else comes second.

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:15.010
Employees can be easily be forgotten in this
never-ending quest to satisfy us.

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:16.740
Here’s how Bezos describes it:

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:20.160
“One thing I love about customers is that
they’re divinely discontent.

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:22.040
Their expectations are never static.

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:23.040
It’s human nature.

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:26.290
We didn’t ascend from our hunter-gatherer
days by being satisfied.”

00:07:26.290 --> 00:07:30.830
This is the hedonic treadmill - no matter
how much our lives improve, our expectations

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:32.240
simply adjust.

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:37.020
Almost any other company would resent this
- constantly having to improve their products,

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:38.830
even the rate at which they improve.

00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:42.590
But Amazon embraces it - they’re just as
discontent as we are.

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:47.270
Except, if there are billions to be made by
concentrating on scale and customers, why

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:48.830
can’t anybody else do the same?

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:54.140
How can a tiny online bookstore do something
exponentially better than Walmart, the world’s

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:58.460
largest company by revenue?

00:07:58.460 --> 00:08:02.980
It's easy to think of the CEO as the supreme leader of a company

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:04.699
but “Even Bono Has A Boss”.

00:08:04.699 --> 00:08:06.550
In this case, shareholders.

00:08:06.550 --> 00:08:11.979
The longer a company loses money, the greater
its risk, and the more anxious get investors.

00:08:11.979 --> 00:08:16.400
But for Amazon - lack of profit isn’t just
tolerated, it’s celebrated -

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:20.990
They could stop and make a dollar, they’d
rather wait and make five, Using profit from

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:24.949
things like AWS to fund projects like Kindle
and Echo.

00:08:24.949 --> 00:08:29.790
Because Bezos is so open about this, shareholders
sign off, and they can think far into the

00:08:29.790 --> 00:08:30.790
future.

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:31.790
Those cargo planes?

00:08:31.790 --> 00:08:32.790
Not so cheap.

00:08:32.790 --> 00:08:34.550
But that doesn’t stop Amazon.

00:08:34.550 --> 00:08:39.889
Supermarkets operate at a 1% profit margin
- but Amazon can buy Whole Foods, and actually

00:08:39.889 --> 00:08:41.149
lower prices.

00:08:41.149 --> 00:08:43.259
It also gives them freedom to experiment.

00:08:43.259 --> 00:08:48.440
The Fire Phone was never ready for, ahem Prime
time, but that’s a small price to pay for

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:49.440
this strategy.

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:55.000
MoviePass, Snapchat, Uber, Spotify, Blue Apron,
all starving companies following in Amazon’s

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:58.100
footsteps, crossing their fingers money will
come later.

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:02.850
Ex-Google CEO Eric Schmidt says Amazon is
already Google’s biggest competitor.

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:07.730
AWS competes with Google Cloud, Echo with
Google Home, Prime and Express, YouTube and

00:09:07.730 --> 00:09:09.339
Twitch Even search.

00:09:09.339 --> 00:09:12.779
A company this diversified will face plenty
of challenges,

00:09:12.779 --> 00:09:18.339
A lot will change in 10 years, but we’ll
always want low prices, fast delivery, and

00:09:18.339 --> 00:09:19.470
easy shopping.

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:22.550
As Amazon conquers one industry after another,

00:09:22.550 --> 00:09:27.959
they may only have one real competitor: the
government, who may say “Do not pass go,

00:09:27.959 --> 00:09:30.430
and do not collect $200”.

00:09:30.430 --> 00:09:35.009
Profits may be shaved, But you and I will
always be able to shave in comfort with Dollar

